OK, here's a fun little idea inspired by the "tombstone wallpaper"
thread. Rather than thinking of it as an extension to the game or some
kind of 'surface map' for nethack, think of it as a nethack manager and
logfile viewer all wrapped up in one cute little package:

It would look a little like a cut-down version of the full game - you'd
have a nethack-like map with all the familiar symbols/ tiles, an
inventory and some of the usual commands (move, pickup, drop, apply,
throw, #chat, #borrow, #sit, #kick, engrave, maybe some others), but
there would be no name/class/role/alignment/gender options. Your
character is just a faceless, generic @. There would only be simple
(and not very random) level/ item/ monster generation, no combat, no
spells, no stats- Just a bland, nondescript generic @ wandering about a
not-very-random-but-otherwise-recognisably-nethack-ish map. Your
starting inventory consists of a wreath or bunch or flowers, maybe a
little gold, a food item (why not?) and not much else.

The map is a graveyard. You enter the South gate, and are told if it is
a full moon, friday 13 etc. Going back out the gate at any time exits
the graveyard program, just like going back up the upstairs in nh
(except when it doesn't, obviously). If you have bones files and hearse
installed on your system then standing near the gate would be another @
- an undertaker. If you #chat to him, the hearse program will be
executed.

Moving further into the graveyard, you see that there are a few random
trees and a whole load of tombstones. You might find a few rocks lying
around, and things like wolfsbane growing in forgotten corners. Perhaps
a small pond or patch of fog here and there, for atmosphere. As you've
probably already guessed, the graveyard's tombstones are generated from
your logfile. By default there would be a tombstone for every deceased
character you have played, but if you wish you could run the program
with filters to commemorate only your best or worst characters, or ones
of a certain class/ alignment/ whatever filters you can dream up. The
inscription on each tombstone gives all the data available in the
logfile for the deceased in a readable format.

Obviously most mature logfiles would create way too many tombstones to
fit on one nethack screen, so there could be North/West "gates"
(leading to a grid of similar "levels") to expand the graveyard as
necessary.

If you have any ascended characters in your logfile, the grounds of the
graveyard will contain temples. An ascended character is represented in
the appropriate temple by a statue. Obviously, if your logfile only has
one ascended chaotic wizard but five ascended lawful wizards, then
Ptah's temple will necessarily be bigger than Anhur's. You can stand on
a statue and #chat to the temple priest for more information: "That
statue represents Nippletwixt, a mighty hero who, after 45840 moves,
now resides immortally at Anhur's side." You get the idea.

Characters from the logfile who escaped the dungeon would be found
roaming the graveyard as peaceful NPCs. You can #chat to them and they
will tell you randomly selected fragments of their story: "I got down
to Dlvl7 before escaping the dungeon with 301 zorkimids." They might
even hang around the temples of their preferred Gods, but I doubt the
priests would let them in, seeing as how they chickened out before
attaining the amulet.

At the east of the graveyard's first screen is the east Gate. it is
marked "This way to the Dungeons of Doom" with some suitably dry and
cynical prediction of grisly demise. Taking this gate closes the
"graveyard" program and kicks off nethack (or slashem, or whatever else
you configure it to.) Just to clarify right now before the outraged
complaints come in, I'm NOT suggesting that the character in the
graveyard has any bearing on your game, so if you go through the
graveyard's east gate with a food ration and a pile of rocks, you do
NOT NOT NOT NOT NOT start your nethack game with those same items.
Nethack starts as normal, you continue a saved game or start a new one
with no data passed on from the graveyard.

Next to the East Gate is a book. Reading the book opens nethack's
guidebook. There should be an inscription somewhere referring to rgrn
as well, and another book containing the rgrn faq.

For every saved game on the system, there is an empty pit. When this
happens you also get a gravedigger (wielding a pick axe/ mattock) who,
if you jump in a grave and #chat, says things like 'That hole is
reserved for dogscoff, a chaotic doppleganger wizard who entered the
dungeon just yesterday morning.'

If you somehow get a pickaxe off the gravedigger, digging up graves
reveals that only the characters who died on Dlvl 1, 2 or 3 have
corpses. The rest were too deep in the dungeon to be recovered and
buried, so their graves are empty. (Because the bones pile gets first
grabs at the grave of any player who dies deeper than Dlvl3)

If you run the program with no logfile/ empty logfile, then you just
see an empty field on a sunny day with lots of trees, and the north and
south gates. Nothing else.

Naturally, whenever you quit the graveyard, the layout and everything
is saved, just like a regular nethack game. (This way you can engrave
yourself messages, and leave wreaths at the graves of your favourite
characters) If you restart graveyard with different filters (and hence
generate a different graveyard) then it's saved under a different
filename.

So come on, is it a good idea? Is it worth anyone's coding time? Most
of the code is already in nethack, it just needs to be recycled. I know
that if I could code, I'd do it.


